{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XZja6dyfzXjmHgPvjfqN2D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven regeneration of Unix utilities in Go, verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/<name>/` - one directory per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - differential testing framework\n- `pkg/sys/` - syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - output formatting\n- `docs/specs/` - SRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n\n- `magefiles/` - build automation\n\n**Current State**: I'm scanning through the releases to identify which use cases haven't been started yet. Release 00.0 has two incomplete items (format and testutils), while Release 01.1 for cat is still pending, though 01.2 and 01.3 are done.\n\nMoving further down, Release 02.0 through 02.2 are all complete, but Release 03.1 has nproc unstarted, Release 04.0 has both utilities incomplete, and Release 04.1 is missing logname. Release 05.0 has echo and tac still pending while nl and fold are finished, with many more incomplete items further down the list.\n\nNow I'm checking the requirement_states section to see how the SRDs map to these implementation statuses.\n\nLooking at the listed SRDs, I notice they start from srd004-ts and skip the earlier ones like srd001 through srd003, which means those earlier requirements aren't marked as \"ready\" and I should only focus on proposing tasks for the SRDs that are explicitly listed with ready status. Let me trace which releases these correspond to so I can identify what work is actually available to propose. Continuing through the remaining requirements, I'm mapping out the rest of the use cases across releases 11.0 through 12.0, with nohup completing release 11.0, then moving into the user management commands for 11.1, and finally the system administration utilities planned for 12.0. I'm noticing there's a discrepancy—some use cases marked as \"not started\" in earlier releases like rel00.0 and rel01.1 don't have corresponding entries in the requirement_states mapping, which means they're not being tracked in the priority calculation even though they should be considered when determining which release to focus on next. Looking at the git log, I can see srd085 corresponds to sync. Let me verify which requirements are actually marked as \"ready\" by checking both the code_status and requirement_states entries for link and sync.\n\nSince sync shows as \"implemented\" in code_status and srd085's requirements don't appear in requirement_states, that means they're complete. For Release 10.0, stat and truncate have all their requirements ready and need tasks, while link can't be proposed yet and sync is already done. The earliest release with ready requirements is Release 10.0, so I should focus there and check the weight budgets from the requirements file.\n\nI don't have access to requirements.yaml given the constraint against using tools, so I'll work with the assumption that each requirement has a default weight of 1 unless stated otherwise. This means I can include up to 4 requirements per task without exceeding the weight limit.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the requirements for each feature. The stat feature has 12 requirements across its ready states, which means I'll need at least 3 tasks to stay within the 4-requirement-per-task constraint. The truncate feature has 9 requirements, also requiring at least 3 tasks to distribute them properly.\n\nThat puts me at 6+ tasks minimum, but I'm capped at 4 total. I need to focus on one SRD first. Since both stat and truncate are new packages that don't exist yet, I'll need to start with a contract task for whichever I prioritize. Let me go with stat since it'