e metadata. Looking at the SRD id pattern and the release description, stat clearly needs pkg/sys.\n\nFor the contract task for cmd/stat, since this is a main package with no exports, the contract is really just a skeleton defining internal function signatures and flag variables as stubs. The constraint says I can't propose implementation tasks for packages without a contract unless they already exist in source_code, and cmd/stat doesn't exist yet, so I definitely need a contract task first.\n\nLet me verify by checking all the releases with \"not started\" use cases to see if their SRDs appear in requirement_states.\n\nScanning through the releases, I'm finding that most of the \"not started\" packages—format, testutils, cat, nproc, ls, logname, echo, tac, expand, cut, sha1sum, sha512sum—aren't showing up in requirement_states, so I should skip those.\n\nContinuing through the later releases, I see the same pattern holding: mktemp, groups, sort, mv, sleep, factor, sha224sum, sha384sum, cksum, sum, and base32 are all absent from requirement_states and get skipped as well. But then stat appears in release 10.0 with the srd082 identifier and is actually present in requirement_states with all requirements met.\n\nLooking further, truncate (srd083) is also ready in 10.0, though link isn't in the requirements. Release 10.1 brings chmod, chgrp, and chown—all marked as ready with their respective identifiers. The pattern continues through releases 11.0 and beyond, but the key finding is that release 10.0 is the earliest one where I have ready requirements to work with, specifically stat and truncate. Since the priority is to focus on the earliest incomplete release from the roadmap, that means I should concentrate on stat first.\n\nI'm breaking down the stat implementation into four sequential tasks: first, I'll create the stub structure for cmd/stat with main.go and basic flag definitions; then implement the core stat functionality covering the main requirements; next, ad